"""Approved narration for Episode 3 (Agni-5 and MIRV), split into beats,
word for word from scripts/ep03_agni5.md."""

EN = [
 ('cold1', 'On the evening of the eighth of May 2026, people along the Odisha coast watched something climb out of the sky above Dr APJ Abdul Kalam Island. And then: nothing. No announcement. No statement from the government, or from DRDO. For a full day, Indian media called it the mystery missile, and guessed.'),
 ('cold2', 'When the Defence Ministry finally posted, it confirmed an advanced Agni missile carrying MIRV, and said its payloads had been aimed at different points spread across a large area of the Indian Ocean.'),
 ('bus1', "Let's do the simple version first, because the acronym is worse than the idea. MIRV stands for Multiple Independently Targetable Re-entry Vehicle. Forget all of that except three words: many, independent, targets. A normal ballistic missile carries one warhead to one place. A MIRVed missile carries several, and each one can go somewhere different."),
 ('bus2', "Here's the picture. The missile climbs out of the atmosphere. At the top, the nose opens and releases a platform carrying the warheads. That platform steers, lets one warhead go, shifts position, and lets the next one go. Each warhead then falls along its own path."),
 ('bus3', "And engineers have a name for that platform. They call it the bus. That isn't a nickname I've invented for you — it's the standard term. Because that is exactly what it does: it carries passengers, and drops each one at a different stop. That's MIRV. Everything after this is detail."),
 ('qs1', 'Four questions, then. How did India build a missile like this, starting from almost nothing? What is Agni-5, exactly? Why would a country that promises never to strike first need many warheads on one missile? And where does this go next?'),
 ('prog1', 'Start in 1983. India has almost no missile industry. The government launches the Integrated Guided Missile Development Programme and puts a rocket engineer named A.P.J. Abdul Kalam in charge of it. Five missiles, one programme. Prithvi, a short-range surface-to-surface missile. Trishul and Akash, two surface-to-air missiles.'),
 ('prog2', 'Nag, an anti-tank missile. And Agni, the ballistic one, which is the family this episode is about. Agni means fire. And the first Agni was not a weapon at all. It was a technology demonstrator, flown from Chandipur in 1989, built to prove one thing: that India could bring something back down through the atmosphere without it burning up.'),
 ('prog3', 'That problem, re-entry, is the hard part of a long-range ballistic missile. It is also why the warhead on one is called a re-entry vehicle. The programme itself was wound up in 2008, once India had what it needed from it. But Agni kept going, and kept climbing.'),
 ('ladder1', 'Climbing is the right word, because the Agni story is a ladder. Agni-1, reaching roughly seven hundred to twelve hundred kilometres. Agni-2, two to three and a half thousand. Agni-3, three to five thousand. Agni-4, four thousand kilometres, but far lighter and easier to move. And then, on the nineteenth of April 2012, Agni-5.'),
 ('ladder2', 'Each rung took years, because every extra thousand kilometres is a harder problem than the one before. You need more stages, lighter materials, better guidance, and a re-entry vehicle that survives a longer and hotter fall. So why does range matter this much?'),
 ('ladder3', 'Because a missile that can only reach your neighbour can only deter your neighbour. Reach decides who has to take you seriously. Agni-5 is the rung where that ladder reaches intercontinental distance, and where India joined a very small group of countries that can build such a thing at all.'),
 ('spec1', 'So what is it? Agni-5 is a three-stage missile running on solid fuel, about seventeen and a half metres long, and weighing somewhere around fifty tonnes at launch. The government describes its range as in excess of five thousand kilometres. Outside analysts estimate considerably more; some say eight. The exact figure is classified, and India has never published it.'),
 ('spec2', 'And how far is five thousand kilometres? Put it in time instead of distance. A passenger jet needs about six hours to cover that. Agni-5 does it in under twenty minutes. Like Pralay in our first episode, it is canisterised: sealed in a tube, carried on a transporter, able to move by road. For a strategic missile that matters even more than it does for a battlefield one.'),
 ('spec3', "A missile sitting in a silo whose location is known can be targeted. A missile that moves, and that can be fired from wherever it happens to be standing, is far harder to destroy before it ever launches. Survivability is the entire point, and in a few minutes you'll see exactly why."),
 ('mirv1', 'Now, the MIRV itself. On the eleventh of March 2024, DRDO flew Agni-5 carrying multiple re-entry vehicles for the first time. The mission was called Divyastra. It went up from Kalam Island, and the official release said that telemetry and radar stations tracked multiple re-entry vehicles through the flight, and that the mission met its designed parameters.'),
 ('mirv2', 'The Prime Minister said he was proud of the scientists. The Defence Minister called it an exceptional success. Then came the eighth of May 2026, the launch we opened with. The Ministry of Defence confirmed an advanced Agni carrying MIRV, and said the payloads had been aimed at different targets spatially distributed over a large geographical area of the Indian Ocean.'),
 ('mirv3', 'Now notice what it did not say. It did not name the variant. It did not give a range. It did not say how many warheads. Analysts estimated, and some suggested this was an early Agni-6. Those are inferences, not announcements, and it is worth keeping the two apart.'),
 ('why1', 'So why go to all this trouble? One word: interception. A missile-defence system works by firing an interceptor at an incoming warhead. Against one warhead, that is one problem to solve. Against a missile that splits into several, each on its own path, the defender has to solve it several times over, at once, in a matter of minutes.'),
 ('why2', 'MIRV is, above everything else, an answer to missile defence. It is a way of making sure that if you ever did have to retaliate, enough of that retaliation would actually arrive. So who can do this? The United States, Russia, China, the United Kingdom and France.'),
 ('why3', "Pakistan says its Ababeel missile has the capability; independent verification is limited, and some analysts doubt how mature it is. India's two tests put it in a group that until very recently was five countries long."),
 ('doc1', "And here is the part that makes all of this make sense, because a weapon only means something next to the rules its owner has set for using it. On the fourth of January 2003, India's Cabinet Committee on Security published the main points of its nuclear doctrine. Three of them matter here. One: credible minimum deterrence. India aims to hold enough to deter, rather than to match anybody's numbers."),
 ('doc2', 'Two: no first use. India says it will not use nuclear weapons first, and that they would be used only in retaliation for a nuclear attack on Indian territory or on Indian forces. Three: if that retaliation ever came, the doctrine says it would be massive, and designed to inflict unacceptable damage.'),
 ('doc3', 'The same announcement set out who decides. The Nuclear Command Authority has two councils. The Political Council, chaired by the Prime Minister, is the only body that can authorise the use of nuclear weapons. The Executive Council, chaired by the National Security Adviser, advises it and carries out its directions.'),
 ('doc4', "And the same meeting approved the appointment of a Commander-in-Chief of the Strategic Forces Command, to manage all of India's strategic forces. That command holds the weapons and carries out those orders, and it is led by a three-star officer drawn in turn from the Army, the Navy and the Air Force."),
 ('frame1', 'Now put MIRV back into that frame. If you have promised never to strike first, then everything rests on being able to strike second: after absorbing an attack, with whatever is left. Mobility keeps the missile alive. MIRV keeps the answer credible. That is the logic Agni-5 is built for, and without the doctrine the hardware makes very little sense.'),
 ('deb1', 'Not everyone reads it the same way, and both sides are worth hearing. The case for: a no-first-use country needs its retaliation to be survivable and credible, and mobility plus MIRV delivers exactly that. The case for caution: analysts point out that a MIRVed missile concentrates many warheads on one launcher, which makes each launcher a bigger prize, and that capability of this kind tends to push neighbours to answer in kind.'),
 ('deb2', 'Writing for the Manohar Parrikar Institute for Defence Studies and Analyses in May 2026, Ajey Lele argued that the test read as a restrained signal rather than a provocation, and that a much longer-range test could have been taken as escalatory by powers well beyond China and Pakistan.'),
 ('deb3', 'Both of those things can be true at the same time. Deterrence is an argument about what other people will believe, which is exactly why serious analysts disagree about it.'),
 ('next1', "What's coming? Agni-Prime, a lighter two-stage missile in the one-to-two-thousand-kilometre class, is in trials. Agni-6 is reported to be under development, with ranges quoted anywhere between eight and twelve thousand kilometres, and notice that word, reported. There has been no official announcement of an Agni-6 at all. Lele's argument is that India may not need one urgently, since Agni-5 already reaches intercontinental distance, and that a longer test would buy more diplomatic trouble than deterrent value."),
 ('next2', 'Meanwhile the MIRV version of Agni-5 is still in flight trials. The single-warhead version is the one in service. As of the fifteenth of November 2026, that is where things stand.'),
 ('three1', 'If you remember one thing, make it three words. Far: officially beyond five thousand kilometres, with outside estimates higher and the real figure classified. Many: one missile, several warheads, each with its own target. And second: this is a weapon built to be used second, never first. That is the doctrine, and it is the reason the other two matter at all.'),
 ('end1', "Agni-5 started as a question about whether anything India built could survive coming back down through the atmosphere, tested off a range in 1989 by a programme that barely existed yet. Next on India's Defence, Decoded: the other half of this story, missile defence, and the shield India is building for itself. To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios."),
]

